# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cador-4-epic-remainder — remaining cador epic work (DB backend, command layer, migration adaptation, conformance, wire host)
#+STATUS: DEFERRED (only the wire host remains, 2026-10-06)
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: epic
#+DEPENDS: cador-3-host-interface-protocol-spec (cador-1, cador-2 closed)
#+BRANCH: cador

* Context

Sub-task 4 (catch-all tail) of the =cador-multidocument-host= epic: the
work that follows once the naming split (cador-1), the multidocument
runtime (cador-2) and the specification (cador-3) are in hand. Kept as one
tracker so the epic's remaining scope is not lost; split into its own
issues as each becomes actionable.

* Remaining work (from the epic §5 Part II, §7.4, §8, §9)

- **Database backend decision + build-out (epic §5.12, §9.3).** cador's
  entity store is today the mock-host in-memory DB with no persistence.
  Decide and specify the target: native CL in-memory DB with DXF
  read/write, native with deferred persistence, or an external DWG library
  (libredwg is already vendored). Recommend + implement.
- **Command layer scope (epic §9.4).** Decide whether =(command "LINE" …)=
  drives native commands in cador or entity access is purely programmatic;
  specify =(command …)= / =(command-s …)= for unimplemented commands. Large
  real-world impact (much AutoLISP drives commands, not the entity API).
- **Migration §7.4 adaptation.** After cador-1's mechanical rename, adapt
  the moved code to the D1 interface in a separate, reviewable commit
  (semantic changes visible on their own).
- **Locking semantics (epic §9.5).** Faithful / checked-no-op / omitted —
  the checked-no-op (detects protocol violations without a real backend)
  is worth serious consideration.
- **Conformance suite (epic §8, D4).** E1–E10 as probes runnable three
  ways (AutoCAD / BricsCAD reference, cador conformance); the D1 fidelity
  matrix is the coverage target; =alfe-test= green in CI against cador with
  no interactive input.
- **accoreconsole / BricsCAD-headless fidelity envelope (epic §9.1, §9.2)**
  — establish the reference boundary with citations.
- **Wire host (epic §1 third host, §9.7).** Specify and name the W2
  display/editor server host. Out of scope for the epic itself; tracked
  here so it is not forgotten.
- **From cador-2 (closed 2026-09-28), deferred by pjb to the wire host.**
  cador-2 built the scheduler, real parking (threads parked on a condition
  variable) and the per-document correlation forest; what needs a wire to
  exist is:
  - the request/response *envelope* and its serialisation (D1 §13.6), with
    the document discriminator (R10);
  - a full *driver / event loop* that serves N parked contexts;
  - a *prompting =host-command=* (a command that asks for input parks);
  - wiring *correlation frames* (=with-host-request=) into the live host
    generics, so nested requests route through the forest.
- **From cador-3 (pjb, 2026-09-28): the host condition hierarchy.** D1 §15
  now specifies a superclass =host-error= with four subclasses
  (not-supported-by-this-host / unavailable-in-headless-context /
  not-yet-implemented / backend-error). Implement it in the runtime and
  re-grade cador's raisers (e.g. =cador-set-sysvar='s read-only path) --
  keeping the existing =code= keywords so handlers that test the code keep
  working.
  *DONE, clautolisp 2.2.146 (2026-09-29).* The classes are in autolisp-runtime,
  and the class is chosen from the code, so every existing raise is re-graded
  without being touched. The raisers are =signal-{host-not-supported,
  unavailable-in-headless,not-yet-implemented,backend-error}= in
  autolisp-host. On cador, pick-mode =ssget= now signals
  =unavailable-in-headless-context=, and =cador-set-sysvar= signals
  =:sysvar-read-only=. Tests: autolisp-host/tests/host-error-tests.lisp.
  Two spec/code divergences (=grread= keyboard, =gr*= drawing not logged)
  are recorded in D1 §15, not changed.

* Progress (pjb 2026-10-05: "keep wire host deferred, implement the rest")

An audit (2026-10-05) found persistence already built (DXF read/write, DWG
through libredwg, the codec registry; D2 §5.12 records the decision) and the
command table at ~105 spellings. The rest, in slices:

- *Slice 1 DONE (2.2.200)* -- the HAL document API D1 §3 left design-ahead:
  host-open-document-from-file (OPEN and Documents.Open use it),
  host-close-document &key save file, host-document-of; and the sysvar scope
  tier (C4): a =scope= slot on every cell, host-sysvar-scope.
- *Slice 2 DONE (2.2.200)* -- locking, the checked no-op of D1 §10:
  host-lock-document / host-unlock-document (nesting count, unbalanced
  unlock = backend-error); changes to a non-current document from
  application context without its lock signal :document-not-locked
  (drawing *drawing-modification-hook*); AutoLISP's COM calls (document
  context) are not checked.
- *Slice 3 DONE (2.2.200)* -- the §7.4 adaptation: the mock identifiers
  renamed (cador-com-object, *cador-registry*, host parameters, the
  MockHost wording), nihil's explicit host-dictobjname, and the C6
  boundary as a test. The COM values "Mock AutoCAD" / "Mock 0.1" and
  PLATFORM "Mock CAD" are behaviour, left as they are.
- *Slice 4 DONE (2.2.200)* -- command layer: an unknown command or a
  failing engine command still stops the sequence without a LISP error, but
  leaves a "; cador: ..." console notice; D1 §7 / D2 §II.13 list the 83
  commands and 24 no-ops as built. Localisation (LIGNE, .pgp aliases) and
  getcname stay with the cadtui-locale-* tickets (they need the localised
  CAD data).
- *Slice 5 DONE (2.2.201)* -- conformance as vendor probes: probe-
  conformance.lsp measured E1 / E2 / E8 / E10 on AutoCAD 2022 and BricsCAD
  V25 / V26 (table in the analysis, section 9). Acted on: the blackboard
  copies (A2 was wrong), BricsCAD drops a symbol value (E1), SDI is settable
  under the BricsCAD dialects (E8); MTFLAGS already matched (E10; AutoCAD's
  core console has none, the AutoCAD dialect models the full product). E3 /
  E6 (a second document's namespace) and E4 / E5 (a live UI) are out of a
  batch run's reach: they need alfe's automation mode driving documents
  over COM, not built.
- *Slice 6 DONE* -- the accoreconsole / BricsCAD headless envelope, cited
  in D2 sections 9.1 / 9.2 (Autodesk OARX 2025 core-console page -- wording
  to re-check, it did not load when fetched; Kean Walmsley's 2012 posts;
  Bricsys V26 startup options /Automation and /B). What no source states
  (get-family, grread, DCL, plotting, startup files headless) stays grade V,
  for the probes.
- *Slice 7 DONE* -- D1 / D2 brought level with what is built: the
  fidelity matrix (open from file, document-of, scope tier, locking,
  dictobjname), DWG read AND write (libredwg writer caveat cited), the
  "still not built" list down to the wire host, the cador-3 acceptance
  items checked (pjb validated D1 / D2 on 2026-10-03).

* Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Each bullet promoted to its own issue when actionable, and this
      tracker updated / closed when they are all spun out.
- [ ] Epic §10 acceptance criteria met once cador-1..4 land.

* Notes

Most of this is gated on cador-1..3 and, for the conformance reference
rows, on live CAD access (BricsCAD/AutoCAD) — hence DEFERRED for now.

* What remains (2026-10-06)

Everything above except the wire host is built (slices 1-7, clautolisp
2.2.197-2.2.201). This ticket now tracks only the wire host (epic section 1,
third host; section 9.7) and the cador-2 items deferred to it: the envelope
and its serialisation (D1 section 13.6), the driver / event loop, a prompting
host-command, and correlation frames wired into the live host generics.
